{"name":"Cliff Blue","hex":"#5d8fbd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cliff","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vertical (or nearly vertical) rock face.","A point beyond which something abruptly fails or decreases in value, performance, etc."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Obsolete form of clef."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cliff"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cliff","blocks":[{"text":"In geography and geology, a cliff or bluff or rock face is an area of rock which has a general angle defined by the vertical, or nearly vertical. Cliffs are formed by the processes of weathering and erosion, with the effect of gravity. Cliffs are common on coasts, in mountainous areas, escarpments and along rivers. Cliffs are usually composed of rock that is resistant to weathering and erosion. The sedimentary rocks that are most likely to form cliffs include sandstone, limestone, chalk, and dolomite. Igneous rocks such as granite and basalt also often form cliffs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG/330px-White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliff"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Trango_Towers_2.jpg/500px-Trango_Towers_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Trango_Towers_2.jpg/500px-Trango_Towers_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Trango Towers in Pakistan. Their vertical faces are the world's tallest cliffs. Trango Tower center; Trango Monk center left; Trango II far left; Great Trango right.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrango_Towers_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg/500px-Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg/500px-Troll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Europe's highest cliff, Troll Wall in Norway, a famous BASE jumping location for jumpers from around the world.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATroll_Wall_in_shadow.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Northern_Areas_40.jpg/500px-Northern_Areas_40.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=th